#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Record a VHS tape and burn a keycaster-style overlay of its keystrokes into the video, in one step.

Usage: produce-video [options] TAPE...   (.tape files, or folders containing exactly one each)

Several tapes are processed one after another. A tape that fails is reported and the rest still run.
`-a`/`--all` processes every tape folder next to this script and implies `--force`, for re-recording
everything after the app's look changes.

Each tape lives in its own folder. Before recording, `screenshots/`, `videos/` and `gifs/` are created in that
folder; `vhs` runs inside it, so the tape's relative `Output` and `Screenshot` paths land there.
The tape is only re-recorded when it changed since the last recording or its .mp4 is missing (`--force`
always re-records, `--skip-record` never does).

VHS's recording of the tape's `Output ... .mp4` is moved to `videos/raw/<name>.mp4` and never modified.
From it come `videos/<name>.mp4` (the recording plus any overlays the tape asks for) and
`videos/<name>-with-keys.mp4` (the same plus the keystrokes; or `-o PATH`). Both videos are then exported to
two GIFs each: `gifs/<name>_large.gif` at 720p with 128 colors, for a blog or anywhere size doesn't matter,
and `gifs/<name>_small.gif` at 960 px wide with 32 colors, for the docs (git ignores `*_large.gif`). Readable
text matters more than compression artifacts, so the GIFs trade colors and frame rate for pixels.
`--gifs-only` re-exports just the GIFs from the videos already there. Last, every image in `screenshots/` gets a
thumbnail beside it named `<name>-thumbnail.<ext>`. Running again with unchanged inputs does nothing;
`--force` re-renders.

Overlays are asked for with directive comments, which VHS ignores:

    #@ footnote bible-livre          show overlays/bible-livre.txt (one paragraph per line) from here on
    #@ footnote "Line one" "Line two"  or give the text inline
    #@ footnote off                  hide it again (it also ends when replaced, or when the video ends)

A footnote is small text on a dark scrim, centered near the top. Each kind of overlay
is one entry in OVERLAY_KINDS, so others (a title and subtitle drawn as a lower third, say) can be added
beside it.

Timing is simulated from the tape the same way VHS runs it: `Type` and key commands sleep for the typing
speed after every key press, `Sleep` waits, and anything between `Hide` and `Show` is not in the video.
Keys pressed while hidden are not shown. `Wait` and `Paste` take an unknown time, so the script warns;
use `--offset` to shift the overlays if they drift.

Needs vhs and ffmpeg/ffprobe on PATH and Pillow (`python3 -m pip install pillow`). Overlays are drawn with
Pillow and composited with ffmpeg's overlay filter, so ffmpeg doesn't need drawtext or libass.
"""

